\name{free_shapes}
\alias{free_ellipse}
\alias{free_rectangle}
\title{
Draw free ellipses and rectangles
}
\description{
\strong{\code{free_ellipse()}:} Draw a centered ellipse on the canvas.

\strong{\code{free_rectangle()}:} Draw a rectangle anchored at its upper-left corner on the canvas.
}
\usage{
free_ellipse(x, y, width, height, mode = "relative", fill_color = NA,
    line_color = "black", line_thickness = 1, line_type = "solid")
free_rectangle(x, y, width, height, mode = "relative", fill_color = NA,
    line_color = "black", line_thickness = 1, line_type = "solid")
}
\arguments{
\item{x}{\strong{\code{free_ellipse}:} Required finite numeric horizontal coordinate of the center in the selected mode.

\strong{\code{free_rectangle}:} Required finite numeric horizontal coordinate of the upper-left corner in the selected mode.}
\item{y}{\strong{\code{free_ellipse}:} Required finite numeric vertical coordinate of the center in the selected mode.

\strong{\code{free_rectangle}:} Required finite numeric vertical coordinate of the upper-left corner in the selected mode.}
\item{width}{Required finite positive full width (not radius), in the unit selected by mode.}
\item{height}{Required finite positive full height (not radius), in the unit selected by mode.}
\item{mode}{One string: \code{"relative"} (default; canvas width and height are 1), \code{"percent"} (width and height are 100), or \code{"pixel"} (logical pixels). Only positions and geometric widths/heights use this unit; font sizes, gaps and stroke widths remain logical pixels.}
\item{fill_color}{Fill color. Default \code{NA} leaves the interior unfilled. An R color name, \code{#RRGGBB} or \code{#RRGGBBAA}; alpha is supported.}
\item{line_color}{Stroke color. Default \code{"black"}; \code{NA} disables the stroke. An R color name, \code{#RRGGBB} or \code{#RRGGBBAA}; alpha is supported.}
\item{line_thickness}{One finite nonnegative stroke width in logical pixels. Default 1; 0 hides the stroke.}
\item{line_type}{One string: \code{"solid"} (default), \code{"dashed"}, or \code{"dotted"}.}
}
\details{
\subsection{free_ellipse}{
The bounding box extends half the width and height on each side of (x, y). Fill and outline are independent; disabling both makes the shape invisible. Coordinates refer to the entire logical canvas, including margins, not to tree or branch coordinates. The origin is the upper-left corner; x increases rightwards and y downwards. Values outside the canvas are not clamped.
}
\subsection{free_rectangle}{
The bounding box extends rightwards and downwards from (x, y). Fill and outline are independent; disabling both makes the shape invisible. Coordinates refer to the entire logical canvas, including margins, not to tree or branch coordinates. The origin is the upper-left corner; x increases rightwards and y downwards. Values outside the canvas are not clamped.
}
}
\section{Similarities and differences}{
Both functions use full width and height and share fill and stroke settings. Their anchors differ: ellipse coordinates identify the center; rectangle coordinates identify the upper-left corner. For an identical bounding box, subtract half the width and height from the ellipse center to obtain the rectangle anchor.
}
\value{
\describe{
